> Now, explain them again like I'm five.
[0mImagine our country is a big playground with lots of kids playing and building things.

* **GDP:** This is like counting all the toys and swings and slides everyone built together! A big number means everyone is playing and building a lot, which is good!
* **Inflation:**  This is when toys start costing more. Imagine your favorite toy used to cost 1 dollar, but now it costs 2 dollars! That means your money doesn't buy as much anymore.
* **Unemployment:** This is when some kids want to play but there aren't enough swings or slides for everyone. They have to wait or go home, which makes them sad.

These things are connected!

* If everyone is building lots of cool things (big GDP), sometimes toys get more expensive (inflation).
* If there are lots of kids wanting to play (big GDP), there might not be enough swings for everyone (low unemployment).
* If nobody is building anything (small GDP), kids might not have anything to play with and there might be lots of empty swings (high unemployment).

The grown-ups who run the playground (government and banks) try to keep everything balanced.

* They can build more swings (spend money) to make sure everyone has something to play with.
* They can make toys a little cheaper (lower prices) so everyone can afford them.
* They can also try to teach kids new games so they don't all want to play on the swings at the same time.


It's a bit tricky, but they are always working hard to make sure everyone has fun on the playground!
